1
00:00:01,200 --> 00:00:07,990
Hello and welcome to the Owl's Lessons! Last time, you learned how to tame luck at the dice.

2
00:00:07,690 --> 00:00:11,870
Today's lesson is very close to my heart: the chouette.

3
00:00:11,570 --> 00:00:15,961
That's French for owl, so yes, it's a game named after me!

4
00:00:20,108 --> 00:00:23,257
Backgammon is normally a two-player game.

5
00:00:22,957 --> 00:00:29,440
The chouette lets three, four, five or more players play around a single board. Let's go!

6
00:00:33,359 --> 00:00:37,506
The idea is simple: one player takes on a whole team alone.

7
00:00:37,206 --> 00:00:41,484
It's the favourite format of clubs and evenings with friends.

8
00:00:42,437 --> 00:00:49,086
Why? Because nobody sits around waiting for their turn. Everyone is in the game, on every move.

9
00:00:49,542 --> 00:00:54,700
And on the board, nothing changes: you play with the usual backgammon rules.

10
00:00:54,400 --> 00:00:57,512
What changes is who plays, and against whom.

11
00:00:59,610 --> 00:01:06,755
In a chouette, there are three roles. The first is the box. The box plays alone against everyone else.

12
00:01:06,905 --> 00:01:13,841
The box wins or loses against each member of the team. It's the riskiest role… and the most exciting.

13
00:01:14,255 --> 00:01:20,139
The second role is the captain. The captain rolls the dice and moves the checkers for the team.

14
00:01:19,839 --> 00:01:23,842
If the team disagrees on a move, the captain has the final say.

15
00:01:23,992 --> 00:01:25,826
Finally, there's the team.

16
00:01:25,526 --> 00:01:31,255
The other players advise the captain, and each of them plays their own game against the box.

17
00:01:33,707 --> 00:01:38,634
How does a chouette start? First, the order of players is drawn at random.

18
00:01:38,784 --> 00:01:42,692
The first player takes the box. The second becomes captain.

19
00:01:42,392 --> 00:01:45,810
The others form the team, in the order of the draw.

20
00:01:46,076 --> 00:01:51,713
Let's take an example with four players: Anne, Bruno, Clara and David.

21
00:01:51,413 --> 00:01:57,126
Anne takes the box, Bruno is captain, and Clara and David advise Bruno.

22
00:01:57,283 --> 00:02:03,179
The game is then played like normal backgammon, with Anne on one side and Bruno on the other.

23
00:02:02,879 --> 00:02:05,766
Except that Bruno never has to think alone!

24
00:02:07,795 --> 00:02:12,416
At the end of the game, the places rotate. It all depends on who won.

25
00:02:13,304 --> 00:02:15,815
If the box wins, they stay in the box.

26
00:02:15,515 --> 00:02:20,353
The captain moves to the end of the team, and the next player becomes captain.

27
00:02:21,160 --> 00:02:24,629
In our example, if Anne wins, she keeps the box.

28
00:02:24,329 --> 00:02:28,656
Bruno goes to the end of the team, and Clara becomes captain.

29
00:02:29,323 --> 00:02:35,972
If the captain wins, they take the box for the next game, and the former box joins the end of the team.

30
00:02:35,672 --> 00:02:40,287
If Bruno beats Anne, Bruno takes the box, and Anne moves behind David.

31
00:02:42,572 --> 00:02:43,718
And the points?

32
00:02:43,418 --> 00:02:49,242
Each team player counts their points against the box, and the box counts them against each player.

33
00:02:49,882 --> 00:02:57,713
With four players, if the box wins a single game, they score one point against each of the other three: three points at once!

34
00:02:58,764 --> 00:03:04,742
But if the box loses a gammon, they lose two points against each player: six points in total.

35
00:03:04,442 --> 00:03:10,297
That's why the box is such a risky role. At the end of the session, the scores are settled.

36
00:03:12,197 --> 00:03:16,049
Now for what makes the chouette so rich: the doubling cube.

37
00:03:15,749 --> 00:03:19,901
In the most common version, each team member has their own cube.

38
00:03:21,121 --> 00:03:26,485
When the box doubles, each team player decides alone whether to take or to pass.

39
00:03:26,185 --> 00:03:28,700
Some may pass while others play on.

40
00:03:29,628 --> 00:03:35,574
For example, Anne doubles. Bruno and Clara take: their game is now worth two points.

41
00:03:35,274 --> 00:03:41,019
David passes: he loses one point right away, and watches the rest as a spectator.

42
00:03:41,169 --> 00:03:45,777
The other way round, each team player can double the box with their own cube.

43
00:03:45,477 --> 00:03:48,015
And the box answers each one separately.

44
00:03:49,290 --> 00:03:53,845
So the box can play a single game with a different value against each opponent.

45
00:03:53,545 --> 00:03:57,238
That's where the twenty-five percent rule gets really exciting!

46
00:04:01,405 --> 00:04:05,332
Why does everyone love the chouette? First, you learn fast.

47
00:04:05,032 --> 00:04:11,418
In a team, moves are discussed, and weaker players benefit from the stronger players' explanations.

48
00:04:12,832 --> 00:04:18,453
Next, it's social: nobody gets bored, and everyone is in the game on every move.

49
00:04:19,014 --> 00:04:26,182
And it's strategic: with several cubes on the table, every decision to double or to take becomes a real puzzle.

50
00:04:29,140 --> 00:04:31,341
A few tips for getting started.

51
00:04:31,041 --> 00:04:37,410
In the box, be careful: you're playing everyone at once, so a mistake costs you several times over.

52
00:04:38,299 --> 00:04:42,373
In the team, give your opinion, but respect the captain's decision.

53
00:04:42,073 --> 00:04:45,414
The captain is the one playing, and has the final say.

54
00:04:45,972 --> 00:04:51,562
Keep the scores in writing, game after game. It saves a lot of arguments at the end of the evening!

55
00:04:53,173 --> 00:05:00,167
Also agree on the rules before you start: whether consulting is allowed, a partner for the box when the team is large…

56
00:04:59,867 --> 00:05:01,756
They vary from club to club.

57
00:05:02,462 --> 00:05:07,633
Finally, brush up on the doubling cube. That's where everything happens in a chouette.

58
00:05:07,333 --> 00:05:10,862
And if you have any doubts, lesson three is made for you.

59
00:05:13,598 --> 00:05:22,941
And there you go, you now know how to play the chouette: the box against the team, the captain moving the checkers, the places rotating, and a cube for everyone!

60
00:05:23,091 --> 00:05:30,123
TickGammon was born from the chouette, and an online chouette is on its way, so you can play with several people remotely.

61
00:05:29,823 --> 00:05:36,745
In the meantime, play for free against other players or against the computer, in your browser, on Android or on Windows.

62
00:05:36,445 --> 00:05:41,877
Next time, I'll show you how to improve against the computer. Subscribe so you don't miss it!

63
00:05:41,577 --> 00:05:43,533
See you soon around the board!

